WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.279 --> 00:00:07.391
Most people assume that experiments in science

00:00:07.391 --> 00:00:10.639
are usually very expensive and are very resource

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:11.580
intensive.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.620
However, this is not always true.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.920
Not all experiments require many resources.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.060
Let’s talk about the work of Alfred Hershey

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.180
and Martha Chase for example.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.519
Did you know that the Hershey and Chase experiment

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.240
was one of the most remarkable works in the

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.000
field of genetics?

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.300
What was the experiment about?

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.640
And what does it help us infer?

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.839
Let us find that out!

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.500
In the timeline of the history of Genetics

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.420
that we looked at, we have reached this point.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.640
So let us step ahead and jump to the year 1952.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.140
Two American geneticists, Alfred Hershey and

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.559
Martha Chase, carried out a very simple experiment

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:56.840
that helped figure out whether “DNA” was

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.949
really the “Transformation Principle”

00:00:58.949 --> 00:01:00.100
or not.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.900
The experiment was quite simple!

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.700
It just required a culture medium, two different

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.920
radioactive isotopes, a mixer blender and

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.460
a centrifuge tube.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.700
A culture medium is a medium used to grow

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.170
different kinds of microorganisms.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.875
With this equipment they carried out an experiment

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.210
using a bacteriophage and a bacterial strain.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.000
Let us find out how the experiment was framed.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.540
Before that, can you tell me what bacteriophage is?

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.000
The term “PHAGE” means virus.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.640
So the name “bacteriophage” stands for

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.380
virus that infects “bacteria”.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.720
So bacteriophages usually infect the host

00:01:43.738 --> 00:01:46.710
bacterium by inserting their genetic material

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:47.980
into them.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.454
And they make copies of themselves using the

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.160
replication machinery of the host bacterium.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.280
The interesting thing here is that the body

00:01:57.291 --> 00:02:00.020
of any bacteriophage is simply made up of

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.740
two components: the outer protein coat and

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.340
the inner genetic material.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.980
Now let us get back to the Hershey and Chase

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:09.760
experiment.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.240
The main aim of their experiment was to find

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.180
out what exactly is the genetic material.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.980
Is it proteins or DNA?

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.880
Since the phage contains only proteins and

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.780
DNA, it is easier to find out which component

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:26.740
gets into the host bacterium.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.700
They simply used radiolabelled markers to

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.540
identify the different strains.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.680
So if the bacterial cells have the radioactively

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.460
marked proteins in the end, then it is the

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.820
proteins which are the genetic material.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.560
On the other hand, if the radiolabelled DNA

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.459
makes its way into the bacterial cell, then

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.320
probably the DNA is the genetic material!

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.670
So the experiment was quite simple!

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:59.160
They first obtained radiolabelled bacteriophages.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.380
One strain had the radiolabelled proteins

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.020
and the other had radiolabelled DNA.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.520
Then they allowed these different bacteriophages

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.980
to separately infect the bacteria.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.540
And at the end, they analysed the bacteria

00:03:14.546 --> 00:03:17.159
to find out which radiolabelled component

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.080
had made its way into it!

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.380
But how did they obtain the radiolabelled

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.720
proteins and DNA?

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.420
What was the process they used?

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.600
And how were the results analysed at the end?

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.420
Let us find that out in the next part!

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.680
Hershey and Chase used bacteriophages which

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.800
are the viruses that infect the bacteria.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.620
We know that their bodies are made up of only

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:48.980
proteins and DNA.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.220
That makes it easier to figure out what gets

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.540
into the host bacterial cells.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.060
The experiment was quite simple!

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.860
They first obtained radiolabelled bacteriophages.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:06.360
There were two different types: ones having

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.840
radiolabelled proteins and the others with

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.800
radiolabelled DNA.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.580
Then they allowed these different bacteriophages

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:16.680
to separately infect the bacteria.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.596
And at the end, they analysed the bacteria

00:04:19.596 --> 00:04:22.650
as to which radiolabelled component had made

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.480
its way into it!

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.740
This is how the simple experiment helped answer

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.520
a few important queries.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.620
Now let us get into the details of

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:33.520
this experiment

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.480
and understand it step by step!

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:38.780
The first thing Hershey and Chase did was

00:04:38.791 --> 00:04:41.210
that they obtained the radiolabelled phage

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.560
strains.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.040
In one nutrient medium they added the radio

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:49.360
isotope Sulphur 35 which is S-35.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.260
In the other, they added Phosphorous 32 which

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.100
is P-32.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:58.420
But why only sulphur and phosphorous?

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:00.980
And that too in separate mediums?

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:05.400
Well, sulphur is an integral part of proteins,

00:05:05.406 --> 00:05:07.960
but is not present in the DNA.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:10.520
On the other hand, phosphorous is present

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.640
in the DNA but not in proteins.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.754
Thus, for easy analysis, they chose the radio-isotopes

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.240
of these two elements.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.440
So one nutrient medium was added with the

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.440
sulphur 35 radioisotope.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.840
The bacteria grown in this medium thus had

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.060
S-35 incorporated into their bodies.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.960
Now what will happen if bacteriophages are

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.199
allowed to infect these bacteria?

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:40.531
It is obvious that S-35 will get incorporated

00:05:40.531 --> 00:05:43.789
in the protein coats of these bacteriophages

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:44.760
as well!

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.940
That is because, these are derived from the

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.020
body of these host bacteria.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:54.340
So their first batch of S-35 labelled bacteriophages

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:55.980
was ready!

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.700
Similarly, in this culture medium, they added

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.560
P32 and allowed the growth of bacterial colonies.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:07.560
As a result, all the bacteriophages obtained

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.640
post infection, had radiolabelled DNA.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.260
So the second batch of bacteriophages with

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:17.880
radiolabelled DNA was also ready with them!

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.820
Now all they had to do is allow these radioactively

00:06:21.834 --> 00:06:24.979
marked bacteriophages to infect new bacterial

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:26.180
cells.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.740
These host cells will be the ones harbouring

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.270
the new phage generations, which will help

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.840
analyse what has passed down the line.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.880
So they infected the bacterial cells with

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:39.880
the two batches separately.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:43.860
After several hours of infection, the cultures

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.580
were agitated in a mixer blender.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:49.340
Why is this step necessary?

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.620
It is needed to make sure that post infection,

00:06:52.633 --> 00:06:55.449
the empty phage coats get separated from the

00:06:55.449 --> 00:06:56.920
bacterial cells.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.080
The agitation helps release the empty phage

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.840
coats attached to the bacterial cells.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:06.340
Now in the next step they centrifuged the

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:08.840
culture at an extremely high speed.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:12.360
Centrifugation process works on the principle

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:13.940
of gravitational force.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.000
So when the sample is centrifuged at a very

00:07:18.006 --> 00:07:20.750
high speed, the heavier, denser particles

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:23.960
settle at the bottom, while the lighter particles

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:25.240
remain on top.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.420
What is settled at the bottom is usually called

00:07:29.429 --> 00:07:32.090
pellet, while the one that floats on top is

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:33.440
called supernatant.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.840
So the centrifugation step helped Hershey

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.200
and Chase to obtain two layers.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:41.880
The one on top was supernatant and the one

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.920
at the bottom was pellet.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.160
They checked the radioactivity in both the

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.640
batches and concluded their analysis.

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:54.040
Before we reach the conclusions, can you help

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.100
Nora guess the interpretation?

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.880
What will be the interpretation if the radioactivity

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.320
is found in the supernatant?

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:06.020
And what will be the inference if it is found

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:06.700
in the pellet?

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:08.480
Think about it!

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.820
Let me help you with this!

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.060
The supernatant contains phage ghosts which

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.880
are the empty shells or coats of phage.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:21.760
It does not have the replicated next generations

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:23.320
of the bacteriophages.

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:27.160
Similarly, the pellet will contain the bacterial

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.860
cells, and the newer phage particles.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:33.311
So now, in case one, if the radioactivity

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.980
is observed in the supernatant, then it means

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.640
that the marker is only present in the protein

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.560
coat of the phage, and did not get into the

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:43.840
bacterial cells.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.980
This can help us conclude that the proteins

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.850
are not the genetic material as the newly

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:53.880
formed phage particles did not receive S-35.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.340
On the other hand, if the radioactivity is

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.970
seen in the pellet, then it would mean that

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:04.420
the newer phage cells have all got the radioactive

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:05.680
P-32 in them.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.980
Thus, the DNA, in the form of genetic material

00:09:09.989 --> 00:09:13.100
has successfully got transferred in the new

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.530
phage particles.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:18.820
This is what the analysis of both the cases can be!

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:23.240
Let us look at the results Hershey and Chase obtained.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.240
On analysing the first sample, they found

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:29.900
that the radioactivity was present in the

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:31.310
supernatant.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:34.140
And in the second batch, the radioactivity

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:36.060
was found in the pellet.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:37.560
What did this mean?

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:39.500
That’s right!

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.059
With this, the two concluded that it is the

00:09:42.059 --> 00:09:44.490
DNA which is the genetic material and not

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:45.800
proteins.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:48.641
If proteins would have been the genetic material,

00:09:48.641 --> 00:09:51.250
then the pellet in the first batch would have

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:54.590
shown the presence of radioactive S35.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:58.388
Instead, the pellet in the second batch showed

00:09:58.388 --> 00:10:00.040
the presence of P32.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:04.160
Thus, it was confirmed that it is the DNA

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.300
which is the genetic material!

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:09.960
With this, Avery, MacLeod and McCarty’s

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.340
analysis that Griffith’s “TRANSFORMING

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:16.220
PRINCIPLE” was DNA, was also proved!

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:20.040
Thus, the long ignored DNA molecule now rose

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:23.380
to fame, and scientists all over the globe

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:26.240
started working on finding its structure.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.360
But how was the structure discovered?

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:32.475
And what does the actual structure of a DNA

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:33.580
molecule look like?

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:38.820
Let us find that out in the next part!

